From 4b15ed0a755074d185e25a6962a77233e772b299 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 06:26:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] metrics: Answer MetricsPort requests from a dedicated
 thread

The MetricsPort is served by the main event loop, so a relay whose main
thread is overloaded, or which has reached its socket limit, stops
answering scrapes exactly when its operator most needs them.

Hand the MetricsPort listener to a worker thread instead, much like
dnsserv_configure_listener() hands DNSPort listeners to evdns. The
worker never touches main thread state: the main thread rebuilds the
HTTP response once per second from a periodic timer, and the worker
accepts one client at a time, checks MetricsPortPolicy, and sends it a
copy of that response. When the main loop is stalled, scrapes still
succeed and get the last response, whose Date header tells its age.

In more detail:

  - The worker is started from the timer, in the main loop, so that it
    runs under the Sandbox seccomp filter, which only applies to threads
    created after sandbox_init().
  - The worker accepts with tor_accept_socket_nonblocking_unlimited(),
    so that scrapes still work when tor has max_sockets - 1 sockets
    open. It holds only one client at a time.
  - metrics_close_listener() runs before a MetricsPort listener socket
    is closed, and calls shutdown() on it: on Linux, a socket that
    another thread is polling stays bound after close(), which made
    rebinding the same port on SIGHUP fail. The worker exits within a
    second once it has no listener.
  - After a failed accept(), as when out of file descriptors, the worker
    sleeps for a second instead of spinning on the readable listener.
  - Windows has no poll(), so it keeps serving the MetricsPort from the
    main loop.

Closes #41458.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
